ct  gets its own  library of our packages because each  project  has its own library you don't have to  worry about updating packages in that  library potentially breaking  your other projects it'll help you make  your projects a bit more reproducible or  portable  because it captures the state of your r  library and the packages installed in  that library  it captures those into a lock file and  that lock file is kind of like  a shopping list of the packages used in  your project  and rm knows how to use that thing to  recreate your library  in the future and that ties into the  reproducibility story you use snapshot  to create that log file  and later use restore to restore your  library and so if you've used packrat  the api is pretty much the same here  now rm tries to prescribe a default  workflow that  just works for the average user but  remains flexible enough  that alternate workflows can still be  built on top of rmd  and so one of the hardest things for me  in building our end  is just everyone has a different  idea in how projects should be managed  how dependencies should be managed  and ultimately rn has to take an  opinionated take on  how to approach this problem that should  at least work for the average user  but still i wanted rn as opposed to  packer to be flexible enough that if you  don't agree with the way that rm does  things  you can still build your own workflow on  top of it  okay and so before we talk about rn i  just want to give a lay of the land of  what it looks like when you're working  on a project without  rn all right so you've started a new  project  you're ready to use dplyr to analyze  some data and so you're going to load  and use in your project you're calling  library dplyr  and so what happens when we actually run  this code  r is going to search your library paths  for a package called dplyr that you've  installed and then it's going to load it  so the natural questions that arise from  that are what is  a library path what are the active  library paths  and how does r search those library  paths when loading a package  all right so let's explore this quickly  so first a raise of hands who thinks  they can tell me what a  library is you don't have to tell me but  just if you think you know what it is  okay fewer hands than i saw for pac grad  which is surprising  but i'm happy to say that the definition  is quite  simple here it is simply a directory  it's a directory in which  packages get installed  nothing more nothing less let any  remaining mystique  be dispelled it's just a directory it's  a folder on your system  it's where packages go to live and get  loaded  all right now where a bit of the extra  complexity comes in is that each r  session can be configured with multiple  library paths that is a bunch of folders  where r will search  for your installed packages the and you  might have seen the dot libpaths  function which is used to  find those library paths so for example  on my system  i've got two of these the first one  you've got  a library path that's in this library  folder and then the other one which is  actually  associated with my r installation  and just to give some terminology for  these libraries  the first one you see is most often  called the user library because it's  your library it's where the packages  that you download  and install go  the last one you have in that list is  the system library  and that's most typically where the  packages that come with r  are installed  and if you're on linux you might see  another set of libraries which are often  called  site libraries and these you can think  of these as the library paths that your  administrator might take care of for you  so if you're in some organization  your our administrator might want to  install some set of packages in the site  libraries  that would then be available to all  users on the system  all right and then next so when r wants  to load  a package how does it search these  things to find it  all right and so what happens is the  first  installation of the package that is  discovered and loaded no size say the  first  so if you've ever say run into a  situation where you have multiple copies  of a package installed say one in your  user library one in your system library  the first one that it finds the one in  your losing your  user library is the one that gets loaded  but if you've ever thought like  wait which where is this package  actually coming from  you can use the find out package to ask  are hey where where is this package that  i want to use where does it actually  live  and so for me d plier i've made sure to  install in my user library  now i should say if you're on mac os you  can actually install  crayon packages directly into your  system library  which i would argue is not a great idea  because  you want to have this separation of  packages that belong to you  that you've installed versus packages  that belong to your r installation  and so mixing them up can cause some  surprises  but this is worth just knowing and  understanding so you just have a  intuition about what happens when a  package is loaded  all right so what is the challenge here  so by default each r  session is going to use the same set of  library paths  so you've got this library it's really  multiple libraries a set of library  paths  and all the projects on your system  unless you're already managing your  library paths in some way  are going to go and use those same  library paths what does that mean  it means that say if you had d plier  0.8.2  in that library that's the package that  you're using in  every one of your projects but of course  different projects can have different  package dependencies  for example suppose project one may be  something you're working on a while ago  that uses d pliers 0.7.8 and that's  say an older version you got everything  tidy wrapped up maybe it's associated  with the paper that you published  and you're thinking okay i'm done with  this it works i don't want to change it  project two on the other hand you're  using some more uh more  modern version of package you've  upgraded to dplyr0.8.2  and say if you're a developer maybe  you're even using the development  version of dplyr in another project  unfortunately in the current state of  the world these projects all share  the same library path so if you were to  install one of these packages you're  changing the version of dplyr that's  used in all three of those projects so  in this world if you were trying to  switch between projects you'd have to go  back and say okay wait i need to go back  and install deployer 0.7.8 for project  one  oh wait but now i need to reinstall  0.8.2 for project two  and you as you can imagine this could  spell disaster  especially if you had no record of which  version of deep plier or its  dependencies  which there are many were used in a  particular project  so the solution at least the solution  posed by rn  is to ensure that each project gets its  own unique library of packages  by using project local libraries you can  rest assured that upgrading packages in  one project will not risk breaking your  other projects  and so as from this idea the use of  project local libraries that the rn  package is born  we give each project its project local  library we make it simple and  straightforward for  our sessions to use that local library  we provide tools for managing the our  installation  or the r packages installed in those  project local libraries  and we try to make that experience as  seamless as possible so  you can use rn without necessarily being  an expert  on rn  okay and so if you've seen packrat this  will feel familiar  once again the api is quite similar but  i'll walk through it  the first step in activating rn for a  project is just calling  rn init and one thing that it does that  is different from packrat is it actually  forks the state  of your current world of our libraries  into a project local library so the idea  is  you're working on a project you have it  working say you haven't  adopted rm as your default workflow or  you don't manage your library pass in a  specific specific way  but you're saying okay the state of the  world is good right now  i want to capture that in this project  so what you want to call is you call  rnvnit  and it takes the packages you have  installed in that library  and moves them into the project library  so it's like forking that state  into your project and so it'll now be  preserved in that project  independently of your user libraries  the other thing is uh infrastructure  related is we create a project local  dot r profile and that basically makes  sure that every time you start r  in that session rn automatically make  sure you use that project library so you  don't have to worry about managing your  library paths rmv does it for you  automatically when you launch r  and this smears what i said before  around  making sure that after you call r invent  you can work exactly the same you were  as you were before with the only  difference being now you have your own  project local library  there are two main differences you'll  see after you call rn  initna project the first you'll see a  nice little banner printed saying like  okay arm has activated this project for  you and this is the version of rm that  it's using  and the second thing is as i've said the  library pass will now be changed  and so these slides are actually using  rn for  its own library and dependence map  dependency management  and so you've got here this giant  library path that lives in the folder  for this project and also this  sandbox library as i'll call it which  we'll talk about later if we have time  but this is basically like a safe  version of your  system library okay  so that is the first goal making it easy  to use project local libraries  the second goal is to make it easy to  save and load the state of those  libraries  or in the parlance of rns snapshot  to save the state of a library and  restore to  reinstall those packages into a library  you can capture the state of your  project library using snapshot  and so when you call snapshot it'll give  you a little  log of what's changing of what is  what dependencies it's capturing when  you  call snapshot so in this case i'm just  capturing three packages for a project  let's say  and it's capturing from crayon the r  markdown packages  or the markdown package the arm markdown  package and the yaml package  and this star is just as a placeholder  to say we didn't know about this before  now we know that using version 1.1 of  markdown now using  2.1 of the r markdown package 2.2.0 of  the yaml package  and i say do you want to proceed yes  please write those and it writes it to  this thing we call the lock file so  we're serializing the state of your  project library to a file called a lock  file which  we'll name on the file system as rm.lock  so this thing is just a text file it's  a shopping list it's the packages that  you had installed  in your library their versions and where  they came from  so a quick look at what that looks like  it's the json file  you can see i've printed a single  package record here for rns  we've got rn version 0.9.2 and in this  case it came from cram  all right so why do we want these lock  files  there's three cases that i that i  outlined specifically here one is for  time capsules this is the case where  you've  okay i'm done a project i want to make  sure that this project still runs a year  from now  to do that i want to make sure i know  what packages i have and i want to make  sure those packages  exist over time so i can revisit later  for collaborative workflows if you're  working with  a set of collaborators you want to  probably make sure everyone is working  with the same environment  if people have different computer  machines one way to make sure you  synchronize the environment is  